</p>
<p>Suppose that this is not the case, that none of the division points are diametrically opposite one another. This means that every arc of length 1 must stand opposite an arc of length 3. Remove one such pair, an arc of length 1 and its opposing arc of length 3. Now we&#8217;ve divided the circle into two large arcs.</p>
<p>If one of these large arcs contains <em>m</em> arcs of length 1 and <em>n</em> arcs of length 3, then the other contains <em>n</em> arcs of length 1 and <em>m</em> arcs of length 3. The total number of arcs of length 1 and 3 in the two large arcs is 2(<em>k</em>&#8211; 1), so <em>n</em> + <em>m</em> = <em>k</em> &#8211; 1.</p>
<p>Apart from arcs of length 1 and 3, each large arc contains only arcs of length 2, which don&#8217;t change the parity (odd or even) of its total length. This means that the parity of the length of each large arc will be the same as the parity of <em>k</em> &#8211; 1. But the length of each large arc is (6<em>k</em> &#8211; 1 &#8211; 3)/2 = 3<em>k</em> &#8211; 2. That&#8217;s a contradiction: <em>k</em> &#8211; 1 and 3<em>k</em> &#8211; 2 have opposite parities. So our assumption, that none of the division points on the original circle are diametrically opposite one another, cannot be the case.</p>
<p>(From Prasolov’s <a href="https://archive.org/details/planegeo/page/408/mode/2up"><em>Problems in Plane and Solid Geometry</em></a>, 1988.)</p>
